> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

> > [...] Puddings heal _slowly_,
> > for pudding farming to be productive enough to save
> > the PC from starvation takes a dungeon level just
> > _stuffed_ with puddings recovering HPs.

> That cannot be true that starvation would be a
> problem; puddings are quite nutritious (250
> points), so I'd just eat one from time to time,

Well, yes, but the issue isn't their raw nutrition
value, so much as it is, their healing rate
expressed as available nutrition value per turn
count.

How often can you afford to kill and eat one
pudding, rather than sacrifice it or let it keep
gaining HPs until it is safe to split it, given that
your total population of puddings is small and their
HP regeneration seems fairly slow, and that you _do_
want to have puddings to sacrifice and puddings to
split, still keep the whole thing a perpetual
exercise without eating all of your "seed corn"? You
might also have to keep a pet fed on the same
economy.

Its sort of like the old story of raising rats to
skin and feed to the cats, raising cats to skin and
feed to the rats, and making your profit by selling
cat and rat skins. Somewhere, that zero input
positive output economy collapses.

Somewhere, an all-pudding-fueled economy must have a
"too small a population to work", too.

Setting most of the dogscoff "pudding mischief
breakpoints" not too far from that minimal
population size would be an interesting limitation
on pudding farming, or at least add a lot of
challenge to pudding farming.

